General elections were held in [[Panama]] on May 3, 2009.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20260114023146/https://www.cia.gov/the-world-factbook/countries/panama/ CIA – The World Factbook – Panama<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref>

==Presidential race==
[[Balbina Herrera]] was the [[Democratic Revolutionary Party]] (PRD) candidate for [[President of Panama]]. She had previously served as [[President of the National Assembly of Panama|President of the National Assembly]] during the [[Mireya Moscoso]] presidency,<ref name=AP51>{{cite web |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-23141697.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140629070920/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-23141697.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=June 29, 2014 |title=Panama Could Have 1st Woman Leader |author=Michelle Ray Ortiz |date=May 1, 1999 |work=Associated Press |via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=4 November 2012}}</ref> and Housing Minister under outgoing president [[Martin Torrijos]].<ref name=USAT /> Herrera won her party's primary on September 7, 2008, defeating [[Panama City]] Mayor [[Juan Carlos Navarro (politician)|Juan Carlos Navarro]] with a ten-point lead.<ref name=USAT>{{cite news |agency=Associated Press |url=http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/news/topstories/2008-09-08-309177159_x.htm |title=Panama's ruling party picks woman for president |author=Kathia Martinez |date=September 8, 2008 |work=USA Today |access-date=November 4, 2012 |archive-date=March 9, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160309015936/http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/news/topstories/2008-09-08-309177159_x.htm |url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Liberal Party (Panama)|Liberal Party]] and the [[People's Party (Panama)|People's Party]] were in alliance with the PRD in support of Herrera.{{Citation needed|date=November 2012}} Herrera was also endorsed by [[Ruben Blades]], a popular salsa musician who had previously run for president and served as Torrijos' Minister of Tourism,<ref name=E59>{{cite news |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-199203124.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924150722/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-199203124.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=September 24, 2015 |title=Super 09; Panama's presidential election |date=May 9, 2009 |newspaper=The Economist |publisher= |via=HighBeam Research |url-access=subscription |location=US |access-date=November 4, 2012}}</ref> and was initially considered the favorite for the presidency.<ref name=EB /> If elected, she would have become Panama's second female president.<ref name=USAT />

[[Ricardo Martinelli]] was the candidate of the opposition [[Democratic Change (Panama)|Democratic Change]], also supported by the [[Patriotic Union Party (Panama)|Patriotic Union Party]], the [[Panameñista Party]] and the [[Nationalist Republican Liberal Movement]]. Martinelli was a successful businessman, and was the chairman of the board of Panama's [[Super 99]] supermarket chain.<ref name="EFE Bio">{{cite news | title = Ricardo Martinelli, el magnate de supermercados que ofrece un cambio al país | language = Spanish | publisher = [[EFE]] | date = April 28, 2009 | url = http://www.diarioextra.com/notigex/show_news.php?subaction=showfull&id=1240959036&archive=1241004167&template= | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20130314215941/http://www.diarioextra.com/notigex/show_news.php?subaction=showfull&id=1240959036&archive=1241004167&template= | archive-date = March 14, 2013 | url-status = dead | access-date = May 23, 2010 | df = mdy-all }} ([https://translate.google.com/translate?hl=en&sl=es&u=http://www.adn.es/politica/20090428/NWS-1830-Martinelli-Ricardo-supermercados-magnate-cambio.html&ei=LlD5S_7wJ8P68AagoKi3Dg&sa=X&oi=translate&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CB4Q7gEwAA English Translation])</ref> During the presidency of [[Ernesto Pérez Balladares]], Martinelli had served as Director of [[Social security|Social Security]] from 1994 to 1996.<ref name=EB/> From September 1999 to January 2003, he had served in the Moscoso Administration as Chairman of the Board of Directors of the [[Panama Canal]] and as the Minister for Canal Affairs.<ref name=EB>{{cite encyclopedia |url=http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/1534506/Ricardo-Martinelli |title=Ricardo Martinelli |author=Anthony G. Craine |encyclopedia=Encyclopædia Britannica |access-date=4 November 2012 |archive-date=25 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121025184252/http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/1534506/Ricardo-Martinelli |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[Guillermo Endara]], former Panamanian president from 1989 to 1994, ran as the candidate for the [[Fatherland's Moral Vanguard Party]].<ref name=CSM>{{cite web |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-199028861.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161018204009/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-199028861.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=October 18, 2016 |title=Conservative supermarket tycoon wins Panama vote |author=Sara Miller Llana |date=May 3, 2009 |work=Christian Science Monitor |publisher= |via=HighBeam Research |url-access=subscription |access-date=November 4, 2012}}</ref>

Though initially the favorite,<ref name=EB/> Herrera was damaged in the election by her links to former military ruler [[Manuel Noriega]]<ref name=E59 /> and by the perception that she was a "Chavista", a supporter of leftist Venezuelan president [[Hugo Chavez]].<ref name=CSM /> Martinelli was also helped by strong support from the business community and his campaign promise of "real change" resonated among poor voters.<ref name=EB/>

On May 3, 2009, Martinelli won the national elections by a landslide, with over 60% of the votes compared to Herrera, who received about 36%. Former president [[Guillermo Endara]] finished a distant third.<ref name="CSM"/> Martinelli was declared the winner after 43.68% of the votes had been counted.{{Citation needed|date=November 2012}} This was the second-largest majority in Panamanian history, and the largest since [[1989 Panamanian general election|1989]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://mensual.prensa.com/mensual/contenido/2009/05/04/hoy/panorama/1771376.asp |title=Cifras, techos y realidades |author=Lina Vega Abad |date=May 4, 2009 |work=La Prensa |language=Spanish |access-date=March 4, 2009 |archive-date=May 8, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090508135744/http://mensual.prensa.com/mensual/contenido/2009/05/04/hoy/panorama/1771376.asp |url-status=live}}</ref> It was also the first time since 1989 that the winning candidate was not a member of the PRD or Panameñista Party. Martinelli's victory was an exception to a trend of victories for left-leaning Latin American candidates.<ref name="E59"/> He was sworn in on July 1, 2009.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.cnn.com/2009/WORLD/americas/07/01/panama.president/ |title=Supermarket tycoon sworn in as Panama president |date=July 2, 2009 |publisher=CNN |access-date=November 4, 2012 |archive-date=March 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304060949/http://www.cnn.com/2009/WORLD/americas/07/01/panama.president/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

